// @test: verify-pass
// @tier: 0
// @level: L1
// @tags: refinement, records, smt, T0994
// @timeout: 60000
// @description: A refinement carried by a record FIELD is a hypothesis the solver can use, exactly as one on a parameter is

// The argument for refinement types is that the invariant lives in the
// TYPE and travels with it. Until T0994 it travelled through a
// parameter and not through a field:
//
//     pure fn triple(a: NonNeg) -> Int ensures result >= 0   PROVED
//     pure fn get(b: Box) -> Int ensures result >= 0 { b.v }  FAILED
//
// `b.v` handed the solver an unconstrained `Int`. The counterexample
// came back EMPTY, which is the tell of an obligation that was never
// built rather than one that failed.
//
// This mattered more than its size: real domain types are records, so a
// refinement surviving only a parameter survives only the shape nobody
// writes.
//
// Both forms are here on purpose. The parameter case is the CONTROL: a
// repair that adds hypotheses must not disturb what already worked, and
// if it does, the substitution is wrong rather than merely incomplete.

type NonNeg is n: Int where n >= 0;

// The control — this proved before the repair and must keep proving.
pure fn triple(a: NonNeg) -> Int
    ensures result >= 0
{
    a * 3
}

type Box is { v: NonNeg };

pure fn get(b: Box) -> Int
    ensures result >= 0
{
    b.v
}

type Pair is { x: NonNeg, y: NonNeg };

pure fn total(p: Pair) -> Int
    ensures result >= 0
{
    p.x + p.y
}

fn main() {
    print("refinement travels through a field");
}
